---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "sorting", label: "Sorting" },
  { id: "selection", label: "Row selection" },
  { id: "filtering", label: "Text filtering" },
  { id: "paging", label: "Local paging" },
  { id: "bulk-actions", label: "Bulk actions" },
  { id: "expansion", label: "Row expansion" },
  { id: "scrolling", label: "Scrolling" },
  { id: "sticky-column", label: "Sticky first column" },
  { id: "column-resize", label: "Column resize" },
  { id: "column-visibility", label: "Column visibility" },
  { id: "dense", label: "Dense" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="data_table" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Wrap one native <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> in a
          <code>&lt;div class="data-table"&gt;</code>. The wrapper scrolls and
          draws the outline, so the table keeps its table semantics.
        </p>
        <p>
          Add <code>numeric</code> to the header and body cells of a number
          column to align them on the end edge with tabular figures. For a
          plain table of a few rows, the <a href={route('table')}>table
          styles</a> are enough.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table">
          <table>
            <caption>Orders</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Customer</th>
                <th scope="col">Status</th>
                <th scope="col" class="numeric">Items</th>
                <th scope="col" class="numeric">Total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td>Shipped</td><td class="numeric">3</td><td class="numeric">$87.00</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Alan</th><td>Delivered</td><td class="numeric">1</td><td class="numeric">$3.76</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Jonathan</th><td>Pending</td><td class="numeric">12</td><td class="numeric">$142.50</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Shannon</th><td>Shipped</td><td class="numeric">2</td><td class="numeric">$9.99</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="data-table">
  <table>
    <caption>Orders</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Customer</th>
        <th scope="col">Status</th>
        <th scope="col" class="numeric">Total</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Alvin</th>
        <td>Shipped</td>
        <td class="numeric">$87.00</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.sorting}>
        <p>
          Put a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> in each sortable header cell. Set
          <code>aria-sort="ascending"</code> or <code>"descending"</code> on the
          header of the sorted column, and the framework draws its arrow. Other
          sortable headers show a faint arrow on hover.
        </p>
        <p>
          Add <code>data-sort</code> to the wrapper to have the framework sort
          the rows too. Pressing a header button reorders the rows by that
          column and moves <code>aria-sort</code>. A <code>numeric</code> header
          compares numbers, so $1,200.00 sorts after $87.00. Other columns
          compare text in the page's language, with 9 before 10. A cell's
          <code>data-value</code> stands in for its text, and a detail row
          moves with its row.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table" data-sort>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending"><button type="button">Customer</button></th>
                <th scope="col"><button type="button">Status</button></th>
                <th scope="col" class="numeric"><button type="button">Total</button></th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Alan</th><td>Delivered</td><td class="numeric">$3.76</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td>Shipped</td><td class="numeric">$87.00</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Jonathan</th><td>Pending</td><td class="numeric">$142.50</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<thead>
  <tr>
    <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending"><button type="button">Customer</button></th>
    <th scope="col"><button type="button">Status</button></th>
    <th scope="col" class="numeric"><button type="button">Total</button></th>
  </tr>
</thead>
`} />
<Code code={`
<div class="data-table" data-sort>
  <table>…</table>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          Each press fires a cancelable <code>expressive:sort</code> from the
          wrapper, with <code>{"{ header, column, direction }"}</code>. A
          server-paged table cancels it and fetches the sorted page. By then,
          <code>aria-sort</code> has already moved. Without
          <code>data-sort</code>, the page handles the header buttons itself.
        </p>
<Code code={`
table.addEventListener('expressive:sort', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  loadPage({ sort: event.detail.column, direction: event.detail.direction });
});
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.selection}>
        <p>
          Put a checkbox in a row's first cell. Checking it selects the row and
          fills it with <code>secondary-container</code>. Name each checkbox
          after its row. <code>aria-selected</code> is not valid on the rows of a
          plain table, so the checkbox carries the state.
        </p>
        <p>
          Put a checkbox in the first header cell to select every enabled
          record visible in the current view. Hidden records keep their selections. It
          shows mixed while only some rows are selected, and it skips disabled
          rows. Every change to the selection fires
          <code>expressive:change</code> from the wrapper with
          <code>{"{ selected }"}</code>, the selected rows. The page can use it
          to update a bulk action bar.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col"><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select all orders"></label></th>
                <th scope="col">Customer</th>
                <th scope="col" class="numeric">Total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" checked aria-label="Select Alvin"></label></td><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td class="numeric">$87.00</td></tr>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Alan"></label></td><th scope="row">Alan</th><td class="numeric">$3.76</td></tr>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Jonathan"></label></td><th scope="row">Jonathan</th><td class="numeric">$142.50</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<tr>
  <td><label><input type="checkbox" checked aria-label="Select Alvin"></label></td>
  <th scope="row">Alvin</th>
  <td class="numeric">$87.00</td>
</tr>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.filtering}>
    <p>
      Put a labelled <code>input[type="search"][data-table-filter]</code>
      inside the wrapper. Mark each searchable column's header with
      <code>data-filter</code>, including headers in grouped rows that span one
      column. The trimmed query matches a substring in any marked column,
      ignoring case in the table's language. A cell's <code>data-value</code>
      replaces its text for search, as it does for sorting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Details stay with their record, and author-hidden records and details
      stay hidden. Clearing the query restores the records without changing
      <code>aria-expanded</code>. Select-all changes only enabled visible
      records; the selection event still reports all selected records.
      The framework reserves <code>data-table-filter-hidden</code> for its
      row state. Do not author it.
    </p>
    <p>
      An optional named <code>output[data-table-filter-count]</code> with
      <code>role="status"</code> and <code>aria-live="polite"</code> receives
      the matching record count. It excludes details and author-hidden
      records, includes disabled records and keeps focus in the search.
      Supply the labels and count wording in your page's language.
    </p>
    <div class="data-table">
      <div class="field"><input id="filter-customers" type="search" data-table-filter /><label for="filter-customers">Search customers</label></div>
      <span>Matching records: <output data-table-filter-count role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Matching records"></output></span>
      <table>
        <caption>Customers</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" data-filter>Customer</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td>Shipped</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Alan</th><td>Pending</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Jonathan</th><td>Delivered</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Code code={`
<div class="data-table">
  <div class="field">
    <input id="customer-query" type="search" data-table-filter>
    <label for="customer-query">Search customers</label>
  </div>
  <span>Matching records: <output data-table-filter-count role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Matching records"></output></span>
  <table>
    <caption>Customers</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col" data-filter>Customer</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td>Shipped</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Alan</th><td>Pending</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
`} />
    <p>
      Call <code>Expressive.DataTables.sync(wrapper)</code> after replacing
      records, changing the query from script or revealing a hidden table.
      Prefilled queries apply on page load. An empty query shows all
      author-visible records; a nonempty query with no marked columns matches
      none. Detail text never contributes to search.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each edit fires a bubbling, composed, cancelable
      <code>expressive:filter</code> from the wrapper before local filtering.
      Its <code>{`{ query }`}</code> is trimmed but keeps its case, including an
      empty string when cleared. For server filtering, cancel synchronously
      before awaiting a response. Cancellation leaves the current rows, order,
      count and focus unchanged. Filter and sort requests cancel independently.
    </p>
    <Code lang="js" code={`
wrapper.addEventListener('expressive:filter', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  loadPage({ query: event.detail.query });
});
`} />
    <p>
      <code>loadPage</code> is your application code. It handles fetching,
      failures and response ordering, replaces records, writes the server
      count and calls <code>Expressive.DataTables.sync(wrapper)</code>.
      Sync never requests a filter or emits selection or sort events.
      After cancellation it skips query matching and count writes, restores
      only filter-owned hiding and synchronizes selection and columns.
      Author-hidden records and their details stay hidden. Sync also works
      inside the canceling listener. The next uncanceled input restores local
      filtering and counts for that wrapper.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.paging}>
    <p>
      Set <code>data-page-size="2"</code> on the wrapper to page local records.
      Use a positive safe integer; absent or invalid sizes leave records
      unpaged. Put named Previous and Next buttons in a named
      <code>nav.pagination</code> inside that wrapper. Ordinary pagination
      links keep their navigation behavior.
    </p>
    <div class="data-table" data-sort data-page-size="2">
      <label for="paged-customer-size">Customers per page</label>
      <select id="paged-customer-size" class="browser-default" data-table-page-size>
        <option value="1">1</option><option value="2" selected>2</option><option value="3">3</option>
      </select>
      <div class="field"><input id="paged-customer-query" type="search" data-table-filter /><label for="paged-customer-query">Search paged customers</label></div>
      <span>Matching records: <output data-table-filter-count role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Matching paged customers"></output></span>
      <table>
        <caption>Paged customers</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col"><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select all customers on this page" /></label></th><th scope="col" data-filter><button type="button">Customer</button></th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select paged Alvin" /></label><button type="button" aria-label="Alvin details" aria-expanded="true">Details</button></td><th scope="row">Alvin</th><td>Shipped</td></tr>
          <tr class="data-table-detail"><td colspan="3">Alvin's delivery details stay with the record.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select paged Alan" /></label></td><th scope="row">Alan</th><td>Pending</td></tr>
          <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select paged Jonathan" checked /></label></td><th scope="row">Jonathan</th><td>Delivered</td></tr>
          <tr hidden><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select private customer" /></label></td><th scope="row">Private customer</th><td>Hidden</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <nav class="pagination" aria-label="Paged customer pages"><ol>
        <li><button type="button" data-table-page="previous">Previous</button></li>
        <li><output data-table-page-status role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Customer pages"></output></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-table-page="next">Next</button></li>
      </ol></nav>
    </div>
    <Code code={`
<div class="data-table" data-page-size="2">
  <label>Customers per page
    <select class="browser-default" data-table-page-size>
      <option value="1">1</option>
      <option value="2" selected>2</option>
      <option value="3">3</option>
    </select>
  </label>
  <table>
    <caption>Customers</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Customer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Alvin</th></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Alan</th></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Jonathan</th></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <nav class="pagination" aria-label="Customer pages">
    <ol>
      <li><button type="button" data-table-page="previous">Previous</button></li>
      <li><output data-table-page-status role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Pages"></output></li>
      <li><button type="button" data-table-page="next">Next</button></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>
</div>
`} />
    <p>
      Add a labelled single <code>select[data-table-page-size]</code> to let
      users change the size. Its positive safe-integer option value updates
      the wrapper's <code>data-page-size</code>, preserving the current page
      when available and clamping it otherwise. For seven records on page
      four at size two, choosing four shows page two with records five
      through seven. Details, selections and matching counts stay intact.
      Disabled or multiple controls, unavailable options and invalid values
      are ignored. Use <code>browser-default</code> for a native select, or
      use the existing FormSelect enhancement. Both work in open shadow roots.
    </p>
    <p>
      Select the option matching the wrapper's initial size. Changing a
      select value from script is silent; dispatch a bubbling, composed
      <code>input</code> or <code>change</code> event to apply it. Alternatively,
      update the wrapper's size and call <code>Expressive.DataTables.sync(wrapper)</code>.
      Sync reads the wrapper attribute. Keep the control's scripted value
      and presentation aligned yourself; DataTables does not refresh FormSelect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Filtering and sorting run before paging. Details consume no page slots,
      and author hiding and expansion stay unchanged. Filter counts cover all
      matching records. Select-all changes only enabled records on the
      displayed page; other selections stay checked, and selection events
      still report every selected record. The framework reserves
      <code>data-table-page-hidden</code> for its page state. Do not author it.
    </p>
    <p>
      An uncanceled filter input or a local sort returns to the first page.
      Canceled requests apply no reset. After changing records or removing
      the size, call <code>Expressive.DataTables.sync(wrapper)</code>. It
      preserves a valid current page, clamps it when needed and emits no
      events. Local paging can slice server-filtered DOM records without
      changing the application's filter count; it does not request server
      pages or use server totals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Page buttons keep focus at boundaries. The framework sets
      <code>aria-disabled="true"</code> and ignores unavailable activation,
      while keeping the buttons in the Tab order. Page changes emit no filter,
      sort or selection event. The optional named status shows localized
      numbers such as <code>1 / 3</code> in the wrapper's language, or
      <code>0 / 0</code> for no records. Supply its accessible name and
      surrounding language. No numbered buttons or page-size selector is
      generated. Standalone pagination stays CSS-only.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["bulk-actions"]}>
        <p>
          Write a <code>.data-table-actions</code> bar just before the
          <code>.data-table</code>, and it joins the table's top edge. It takes
          the selected color while any row is selected. A leading label, such
          as a count, pushes the buttons to the end. The page keeps the count
          current and decides which buttons apply. Add <code>hidden</code> to
          remove the bar.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="data-table-actions">
  <span>2 selected</span>
  <button type="button" class="button text">Archive</button>
  <button type="button" class="button text">Delete</button>
</div>
<div class="data-table">
  <table>…</table>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.expansion}>
        <p>
          Put a <code>tr.data-table-detail</code> right after a row. It shows
          only while that row holds a button with
          <code>aria-expanded="true"</code>. The page flips the attribute, and
          the framework shows the row and turns the button's icon. Put the
          toggle in a <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>, because the framework draws a
          button in a <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> as a sort control. Give the
          detail cell a <code>colspan</code> across the table.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<tr>
  <th scope="row">Order 1042</th>
  <td class="numeric">$87.00</td>
  <td>
    <button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="order-1042" aria-label="Details for order 1042">
      <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
    </button>
  </td>
</tr>
<tr id="order-1042" class="data-table-detail">
  <td colspan="3">Two items, shipped Monday.</td>
</tr>
`} />
<Code code={`
toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
  toggle.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(toggle.getAttribute('aria-expanded') !== 'true'));
});
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.scrolling}>
        <p>
          Give a long table a <code>max-block-size</code>. Once the wrapper
          scrolls, the header row sticks to its top. A wrapper that scrolls
          must be reachable from the keyboard, so give it
          <code>tabindex="0"</code>, <code>role="region"</code> and a name,
          usually the caption.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="scroll-caption" style="max-block-size: 240px;">
          <table>
            <caption id="scroll-caption">Monthly visitors</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col" class="numeric">Visitors</th><th scope="col" class="numeric">Change</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">January</th><td class="numeric">12,480</td><td class="numeric">+4%</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">February</th><td class="numeric">11,902</td><td class="numeric">−5%</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">March</th><td class="numeric">13,377</td><td class="numeric">+12%</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">April</th><td class="numeric">13,015</td><td class="numeric">−3%</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">May</th><td class="numeric">14,260</td><td class="numeric">+10%</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">June</th><td class="numeric">15,104</td><td class="numeric">+6%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="data-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="visitors-caption"
     style="max-block-size: 240px">
  <table>
    <caption id="visitors-caption">Monthly visitors</caption>
    …
  </table>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["sticky-column"]}>
        <p>
          Add <code>sticky-column</code> to the wrapper of a wide table to keep
          its first column in view while the other columns scroll sideways.
          When the first column holds row checkboxes, the name cell after it
          stays pinned too, and the checkbox column takes the fixed width of
          <code>--md-comp-data-table-checkbox-column-width</code>. Change that
          token, not the cell, if you restyle the checkbox column. Pinned cells
          keep their row's hover and selected colors, and a divider marks
          their edge.
        </p>
        <p>
          Give each row one cell in the pinned column: a first cell that spans
          several columns pins its whole width. Detail rows and the caption
          scroll with the table.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table sticky-column" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="accounts-caption">
          <table style="min-inline-size: 960px;">
            <caption id="accounts-caption">Accounts</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col"><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select all accounts"></label></th><th scope="col">Account</th><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col" class="numeric">Seats</th><th scope="col">Region</th><th scope="col">Renews</th><th scope="col">Owner</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Northwind"></label></td><th scope="row">Northwind</th><td>Business</td><td class="numeric">120</td><td>eu-west</td><td>2027-03-01</td><td>Ada Lovelace</td></tr>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Contoso"></label></td><th scope="row">Contoso</th><td>Enterprise</td><td class="numeric">860</td><td>us-east</td><td>2026-12-15</td><td>Grace Hopper</td></tr>
              <tr><td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Fabrikam"></label></td><th scope="row">Fabrikam</th><td>Team</td><td class="numeric">24</td><td>ap-south</td><td>2027-06-30</td><td>Edsger Dijkstra</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="data-table sticky-column" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="accounts-caption">
  <table>
    <caption id="accounts-caption">Accounts</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col"><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select all accounts"></label></th>
        <th scope="col">Account</th>
        <th scope="col">Plan</th>
        …
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><label><input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select Northwind"></label></td>
        <th scope="row">Northwind</th>
        <td>Business</td>
        …
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["column-resize"]}>
        <p>
          End a header cell in the first header row with a
          <code>&lt;button type="button" class="drag-handle"&gt;</code> named
          after its column, and people can resize that column. They drag the
          handle, or focus it and press the arrow toward the column's end edge
          to widen it by 16px (64px with Shift) and the other arrow to narrow
          it. Home and End jump to the narrowest and widest width, and a click,
          Enter or Space steps through narrow, middle and wide, the
          single-pointer alternative to dragging. The keys mirror in a
          right-to-left page.
        </p>
        <p>
          A column stays between
          <code>--md-comp-data-table-column-min-width</code> and the wrapper's
          inner width. The first resize holds every column at its current
          width, and the table scrolls sideways once its columns outgrow the
          wrapper. The handle is not a sort control, so a header cell can hold
          both. Put a handle only in a cell that spans one column, and under
          <code>sticky-column</code> leave the checkbox column without one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each change fires <code>expressive:resize</code> from the wrapper
          with <code>{"{ header, column, width }"}</code>. The framework stores
          nothing: save widths from the event and restore them with
          <code>Expressive.DataTables.setWidth(header, px)</code>, which fires
          no event.
        </p>
        <div class="data-table" data-sort>
          <table>
            <thead>
